// The wavefront schedule: the path integrator's steps advanced breadth first,
// through a queue, as MoonRay and pbrt's own wavefront integrator run them.
// Scalar still -- one path step at a time -- so this is the scalar schedule
// with one decision changed: how the path recursion is run. scalar.bonsai
// runs `full_path_step` depth first, its tail call a back edge, one sample's
// whole path before the next sample starts. This runs it breadth first: a
// step that continues its path is an entry on a queue, and once every sample
// of the pixel has taken its first step the queue is drained, a round per
// bounce, every live path of the pixel advancing together. The queue is the
// pixel's -- one per iteration of `p`, sized by the sample count -- and its
// drain is a parfor named `paths`, for a later directive to make a gang of.
//
// What changes is the order a pixel's samples are summed in: by bounce rather
// than by sample, so the film differs from scalar.bonsai's by float
// reassociation and no more. compare.sh checks it within a tolerance for that
// reason.
schedule {
    walk_step.loopify();
    path_step.loopify();

    paths = render.queue(p);
    full_path_step.defer(full_path_step, paths);

    trace.sort(primitives.Interior,
               |i : u8, r : Ray, axis : u8|
               select((1.0 / r.d)[axis] < 0.0,
                      1.0 - cast[[Float]](i), cast[[Float]](i)))
         .sort(Inst.blas.Interior,
               |i : u8, r : Ray, axis : u8|
               select((1.0 / r.d)[axis] < 0.0,
                      1.0 - cast[[Float]](i), cast[[Float]](i)))
         .loopify(64);
    trace_any.loopify(64);

    render.bind(p, CPUThread);
}
